{"title": "Two Kinds of Quiet", "chapter": "The Acoustics of a Marriage", "summary": "Daniel Okafor's central diagnostic: restorative quiet, two people comfortable in companionable silence, versus punishing quiet, where silence is the sentence imposed after every crime of speech. The first kind refuels a marriage; the second starves it while looking respectable to visitors. Couples learn to audit which quiet fills their evenings, because the treatment differs completely: one needs protection from the world's noise, the other needs thawing, carefully, at the exact points where speech froze.", "example": "A Lagos couple proud of never fighting described evenings of parallel silence with separate screens. In therapy they discovered their last real conversation predated their second child. House quiet, marriage frozen: Daniel Okafor's case study gives the pattern its name and its first thaw, a nightly ten minutes of actual talk.", "action": "Audit this week's evenings honestly: restorative or punishing? If punishing, install ten minutes of real talk nightly for two weeks before judging the marriage."},
{"title": "The Sentence You Believe Is Unsafe", "chapter": "Where Speech Froze", "summary": "Every frozen house contains specific unsayable sentences: I am lonely beside you, I regret the move, I do not desire this life, I am failing. Daniel Okafor's method maps the unsayables, then builds the smallest safe version of each, spoken with a therapist's grammar: my experience, my need, my request. The book's claim is hopeful: marriages rarely need new partners; they need the frozen sentences thawed at a survivable temperature, one degree at a time.", "example": "A husband whose unsayable was I never wanted the family business carried it for nineteen years as irritability. Spoken at last, in the grammar, it reorganised the marriage in a month: his wife had suspected an affair for a decade. The truth, however heavy, weighed less than the suspicion.", "action": "Write your unsayable sentence privately. Then write its smallest survivable version, experience, need, request, and choose its moment this month."},
{"title": "Speaking Volume: The Repair Rituals", "chapter": "Thawing the House", "summary": "The manual's closing toolkit: a daily ten minute exchange with phones elsewhere, a weekly state of the union with an agenda of three kind questions, and the rule that repairs be attempted within a day, however clumsily. Daniel Okafor insists the rituals look too small because we mistake marriage for a renovation; it is plumbing. The couples who keep the small rituals report that big conversations eventually become possible at ordinary volume, which is the whole definition of a house that is quiet in the good way.", "example": "One couple's state of the union is three questions every Sunday: what did I do that helped, what hurt, what do you need this week. Nine minutes, timed by their daughter's bath. They credit it with ending their frozen years more than the therapy that taught it, because the ritual outlived the therapist.", "action": "Install the Sunday three questions this week: helped, hurt, need. Nine minutes, timed, phones elsewhere."}
