{"title": "The Man Who Kept Naming Through Every Disaster", "chapter": "David Starr Jordan's Collections", "summary": "David Starr Jordan's specimens were destroyed by lightning, fire and the 1906 earthquake, which shattered his life's jars in a single morning. Each time he rebuilt, re-collected, re-labelled, eventually sewing name tags directly onto fish so no disaster could separate them again. -Yo Ma reads his persistence with admiration and unease: the same will that rebuilds a science can harden into the refusal to admit the whole scheme is wrong. Both readings become necessary as his story darkens.", "example": "After the earthquake he walked the ruined museum identifying fish on the floor from memory, an act of heroic scholarship and, in -Yo Ma's telling, a warning. The labels went onto the bodies themselves thereafter: order would survive the next disaster by being stitched to the world.", "action": "Name one filing system in your life you keep repairing instead of questioning. Schedule its honest review this month."},
{"title": "Fish Do Not Exist, and That Is Good News", "chapter": "The Taxonomic Verdict", "summary": "Modern cladistics shows a lungfish is closer kin to a cow than to a salmon; the class fish groups creatures by our convenience, water, fins, scales, not by their actual family tree. -Yo Ma receives this personally: the categories that organised her suffering, success and failure, normal life, broken person, were also conveniences mistaken for facts. Dissolving fish becomes a permission slip: revise the taxonomy of your own life when it stops matching the specimens in front of you.", "example": "She describes telling people at parties that fish do not exist and watching the productive discomfort spread. The discomfort is the book in miniature: a filing error corrected feels like loss before it feels like freedom, in ichthyology and in biography alike.", "action": "Write down one label you live under that no longer matches the specimen. Draft its replacement in pencil."},
{"title": "David Starr Jordan's Shadow: Order in Service of Cruelty", "chapter": "The Eugenics Chapters", "summary": "-Yo Ma does not let her hero keep his halo: David Starr Jordan was a leading eugenicist whose categories of human fitness fed real policy and real suffering. The book's hardest argument follows: the drive to order is not innocent; misapplied to people it becomes a machinery of ranking. The same man who sewed labels onto fish to defeat chaos helped label human beings to defeat mercy. Remembering both is the reader's assignment, and the reason the book belongs in science and ethics courses alike.", "example": "She visits the archives and finds his handwriting on reports that shaped sterilisation laws, in the same hand that labelled specimens with such care. The juxtaposition is the book's moral centre: reverence for order, unchecked by humility about categories, scales from jars to atrocities without changing its handwriting.", "action": "Audit one ranking you participate in, at work or online, for what it does to the bottom of the list. Change one practice this month."}
